language/functions.md:
> 💡 **Tip:** If a function returns `^Type`, you can dereference at the call site with `^` to get the value directly without storing the pointer:
> ```gray
> const Foo struct {
>     n i64
> }
>
> do something() -> ^Foo {
>     mut f = new(Foo)
>     return f
> }
>
> do main() {
>     mut f = something()^   // dereference at call site, f is Foo, not ^Foo
>     println(f)
> }
> ```

A function that returns `^Type` can be dereferenced at the call site with `^`, which gives the value directly without storing the pointer:

```gray
const Foo struct {
    n i64
}

do something() -> ^Foo {
    mut f = new(Foo)
    return f
}

do main() {
    mut f = something()^   // dereference at call site, f is Foo, not ^Foo
    println(f)
}
```

language/statements.md:
> 💡 **Tip:** You can iterate over an inline array literal directly — no variable needed:
> ```gray
> do main() {
>     for_each item in {"a", "b", "c"} {
>         println(item)
>     }
> }
> ```
> This works with any element type and supports the index form (`for_each i, item in {...}`) as well.

A for-each loop can iterate over an inline array literal directly, with no variable needed:

```gray
do main() {
    for_each item in {"a", "b", "c"} {
        println(item)
    }
}
```
This works with any element type and supports the index form (`for_each i, item in {...}`) as well.
